research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl-24-{"id":"ex-limsup-of-alternating-sequence","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter computes liminf=-1 and limsup=1 for the alternating sign sequence and uses their inequality to show nonconvergence. The local zero-based sign convention changes which parity attains each value without changing the mathematical content; the verification is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl-25-{"id":"ex-subsequential-limits-fill-unit-interval","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Enumerating increasingly fine rational grids in [0,1] so that the finite subsequential-limit set is exactly [0,1] is a standard textbook construction. Searches found the equivalent enumeration-of-rationals construction but not this exact triangular block sequence; it is recoverable established mathematics rather than positively novel.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl-26-{"id":"ex-ratio-fails-root-succeeds","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Periodically perturbing a geometric sequence so its roots converge while consecutive ratios oscillate is a standard counterexample pattern, and UCI gives closely related alternating-base examples, but no semantically identical source for the exact exponent -k+(-1)^k surfaced. The explicit computation is local.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-real-analysis-limits.provenance.jsonl-27-{"id":"ex-strict-ratio-root-chain","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.uci.edu/~ndonalds/math140a/notes.pdf"],"rationale":"UCI gives the same alternating-base construction 2^{-n} on even indices and 3^{-n} on odd indices, computes root values 1/2 and 1/3, and ratio liminf 0 and limsup infinity. The local version expresses parity through its canonical alternating sequence and expands the sourced computation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
