research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-378-[L6] The product $0 \cdot (\pm\infty)$ is left undefined in $\overline{\mathbb{R}}$ ([[def-extended-reals]]).
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-379-
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-380-## Counterexample
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-381-
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-382-**Proof technique:** direct.
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-383-
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-384-1.1 The sequence $x_k = 1/\iota(k+1)$ is well defined, positive, and converges to $0$: given a real $\varepsilon > 0$, take a natural $p \ge 1$ with $1/p < \varepsilon$; for $k \ge p$ we have $\iota(k+1) > \iota(p) > 0$, hence $0 < x_k < 1/p < \varepsilon$. [given, L1, L2, L3]
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-385-
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-386-1.2 For every real $c > 0$ the sequence $y^{(c)}_k = c\,\iota(k+1)$ diverges to $+\infty$: given a real $M$, the quotient $M/c$ is real, so there is a natural $p \ge 1$ with $M/c < \iota(p)$, and for $k \ge p$ we get $\iota(k+1) > \iota(p) > M/c$, hence $y^{(c)}_k = c\,\iota(k+1) > M$ after multiplying by $c > 0$. [given, L1, L2, L3, L5]
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-387-
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-388-1.3 The sequence $z_k = \iota(k+1)\iota(k+1)$ diverges to $+\infty$: given a real $M$, take a natural $p \ge 1$ with $M < \iota(p)$; for $k \ge p$ we have $\iota(k+1) \ge 1$ and $\iota(k+1) > \iota(p) > M$, so $z_k \ge \iota(k+1) > M$. [given, L1, L2, L3, L5]
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-389-
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md:390:2.1 For every real $c > 0$ the product sequence is constant: $x_k y^{(c)}_k = \big(1/\iota(k+1)\big)\,c\,\iota(k+1) = c$ for every $k$, so it converges to $c$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, L3, L5]
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-391-
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-392-2.2 The product with $(z_k)$ is $x_k z_k = \big(1/\iota(k+1)\big)\iota(k+1)\iota(k+1) = \iota(k+1)$, which diverges to $+\infty$ by the argument of step 1.3 with the single factor, and therefore converges to no real number. [step 1.1, step 1.3, L1, L2, L3, L4, L5]
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-393-
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-394-3.1 Now take the three pairs $(x, y^{(1)})$, $(x, y^{(2)})$ and $(x, z)$. In each, the first sequence is null and the second diverges to $+\infty$, so each pair satisfies the hypotheses of the refuted claim; but the three products converge to $1$, converge to $2$, and converge to $+\infty$ in the extended sense. Since $1 \ne 2$ and limits are unique, no single $v \in \overline{\mathbb{R}}$ describes all three, and the claim is false. [step 2.1, step 2.2, L4, L5, L6] ∎
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-395-
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-396-## Remarks
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-397-
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-398-- **This is why the entry is blank in the table.** [[def-extended-reals]] leaves $0 \cdot (\pm\infty)$ undefined not out of caution but because any value assigned to it would make some instance of a product rule false, and the three pairs above already realise three different behaviours.
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-399-
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-400-- **The same phenomenon rules out $(+\infty) + (-\infty)$.** Taking $a_k = \iota(k)$ and $b_k = -\iota(k)$ gives a sum that is constantly $0$, while $a_k = \iota(k)$ and $b_k = -\iota(k) - \iota(k)$ gives a sum diverging to $-\infty$; both pairs have $a_k \to +\infty$ and $b_k \to -\infty$.
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-401-
research/audit/wave6-dispatch/certifier-cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate.prompt.md-402-- **Measure theory's convention is not a counterexample to this.** Texts that set $0 \cdot \infty := 0$ are fixing the value of a *formula* in a context where the factor $0$ is the measure of a null set, not asserting a limit rule; the distinction is spelled out in [[rem-extended-real-conventions]].
--
